//! The build half of libcurl's runtime-loading route: three modules and one
//! generated file.
//!
//! An SDK that ships the runtime route calls `createModule` in place of
//! `translate.createModule()` and imports what comes back as its `c` module.
//! Nothing here links the native library. That is the point: in this
//! configuration the symbols arrive through the platform loader at run time,
//! and linking the archive as well would make the linker demand them before
//! `load` ever runs.
//!
//!   rt        runtime/loader.zig, the reusable core. Pure Zig; imported by the
//!             facade in both configurations, because `LoadSource` and
//!             `LoadError` are this SDK's public types and must be the same
//!             types whichever configuration is built.
//!   library   runtime/library.zig, this SDK's descriptor: the namespaced
//!             synchronization binding and the one load-time check.
//!   dispatch  generated by runtime/generate.zig from the real translation
//!             output: the table, the wrappers, `load`, and the re-exported
//!             translated namespace. It imports `rt`, `library` and the
//!             generator's patched translation as `c_native`, which imports the
//!             dispatch back as `c_routed`.
//!   sync.c    compiled into the dispatch with `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=curl_rt`, so
//!             its symbols cannot collide with another SDK's.
//!
//! ## The one thing a caller must not do
//!
//! Do not link the compiled C archive into a module that imports this dispatch.
//! The facade's `c` import is the dispatch, and nothing else in the graph should
//! reference the library, or the linker resolves the symbols statically and the
//! runtime route silently becomes a linked one.
//!
//! ## What is routed, and what is not
//!
//! The translated header for this SDK is `zig/translate.h`, which includes
//! `<curl/curl.h>` and defines no macro. `include/curl/curl.h` is the whole of
//! curl's public surface: `:62-63` open `curlver.h` and `system.h`, `:3341-3347`
//! open `easy.h`, `multi.h`, `urlapi.h`, `options.h`, `header.h`,
//! `websockets.h` and `mprintf.h`, and `:3350-3354` open `typecheck-gcc.h` on a
//! C compiler new enough and with type checking enabled.
//!
//! ### Required: the 100 names `curl.h` and its siblings declare
//!
//! Those headers declare exactly **100** functions, and all 100 are routed. The
//! count is the two sets agreeing with nothing left over on either side:
//! `lib/libcurl.def` is upstream's own export list for the Windows DLL and holds
//! 100 names after its `EXPORTS` line, and they are the same 100.
//!
//! No name is optional, and that is the part of curl's surface that had to be
//! read out of the C sources rather than assumed. Twenty-six of the 100 are
//! behind a `CURL_DISABLE_*` macro in the unit that defines them, so the
//! obvious classification would be "optional"; it is wrong because every one of
//! those families defines the symbol again in the `#else` branch:
//!
//!   * `curl_ws_send`, `curl_ws_recv`, `curl_ws_start_frame`, `curl_ws_meta`
//!     inside `lib/ws.c:28` `#ifndef CURL_DISABLE_WEBSOCKETS`, stubbed at
//!     `lib/ws.c:2027-2071`;
//!   * `curl_easy_header`, `curl_easy_nextheader` inside `lib/headers.c:30`
//!     `!CURL_DISABLE_HTTP && !CURL_DISABLE_HEADERS_API`, stubbed at
//!     `lib/headers.c:396-425`;
//!   * `curl_easy_option_by_name`, `curl_easy_option_by_id`,
//!     `curl_easy_option_next` inside `lib/easygetopt.c:28`
//!     `#ifndef CURL_DISABLE_GETOPTIONS`, stubbed at `lib/easygetopt.c:78-97`;
//!   * the twelve `curl_mime_*` inside `lib/mime.c:39-41`, stubbed at
//!     `lib/mime.c:2179-2282`;
//!   * `curl_formadd`, `curl_formget`, `curl_formfree` inside
//!     `lib/formdata.c:30`, stubbed at `lib/formdata.c:842-864`;
//!   * `curl_pushheader_bynum`, `curl_pushheader_byname` inside `lib/http2.c:26`
//!     `!CURL_DISABLE_HTTP && USE_NGHTTP2`, stubbed at `lib/http2.c:2981-2997`.
//!
//! `curl_pushheader_*` is the case that makes this worth stating. This package
//! defines `USE_MBEDTLS` and nothing else, and `build.zig.zon` has exactly one
//! dependency, mbed-TLS, so `USE_NGHTTP2` cannot be set here. Routing those two
//! as required would make this package's own shared object fail this package's
//! own load. They are routed because upstream exports them whatever the
//! configuration, and a provider that genuinely lacks one is a build-time
//! decision to record here, not a guess.
//!
//! The two `CURL_NO_OLDIES` spellings never change this either.
//! `include/curl/curl.h:2294-2324` is the last block that macro guards and the
//! earliest `CURL_EXTERN` in the file is `curl_strequal` at `:2453`, so no
//! declared function sits inside one. The deprecated spellings themselves -
//! `curl_escape`, `curl_unescape`, `curl_multi_socket`, `curl_multi_socket_all`,
//! `curl_formadd`, `curl_mime_data` - are declared before their macros and are
//! exported by `lib/libcurl.def`, so they are routed like any other name.
//!
//! One more reason the routed set is exactly those 100 and not more: a macro
//! whose name is already a declaration is not translated at all. The pinned
//! compiler translates top-level declarations first and macros after
//! (`lib/compiler/translate-c/Translator.zig:287` then `:292`), skipping any
//! macro already in scope at `:4259-4260`. That is why `curl_easy_setopt` and
//! `curl_multi_socket` reach the translation as the single extern declaration
//! their headers give, and why the type checker in `typecheck-gcc.h` adds
//! nothing on top of them.
//!
//! `provider_optional` and `provider_excluded` below are therefore empty, and
//! are written out rather than omitted so that adding a header with an optional
//! or deliberately-direct entry point is a change in this file with a name in
//! it.
//!
//! ### Direct: the C library's own surface, which curl pulls in heavily
//!
//! curl's headers open the C library where most other SDKs open almost none of
//! it. `include/curl/curl.h:65-66` opens `<stdio.h>` and `<limits.h>`, `:74-75`
//! opens `<sys/types.h>` and `<time.h>` - the comment at `:73` is "The include
//! stuff here below is mainly for time_t!" - `:70` adds `<sys/param.h>` on
//! FreeBSD and MidnightBSD, `:90-100` adds `<sys/select.h>` on a documented
//! list of platforms and feature-test macros, and `:102-105` adds
//! `<sys/socket.h>` and `<sys/time.h>` off Windows.
//! `include/curl/mprintf.h:27-28` adds `<stdarg.h>` and `<stdio.h>` again, for
//! the variadic `va_list` and for `FILE *`. `include/curl/curl.h:77-85` puts
//! `<winsock2.h>` and `<ws2tcpip.h>` in the Windows closure.
//!
//! A translation step emits a declaration for every function it parses in that
//! closure, whether or not this build calls it, so the translation carries the
//! target C library's whole reachable surface alongside curl's own. Those are
//! the C library's symbols, not the provider's: a `libcurl.so` does not export
//! `printf`, and asking a provider for one would either fail the load outright
//! or, worse, resolve it out of the C library the provider itself depends on.
//! They are therefore classified as noise, which keeps the declarations direct.
//!
//! **That list is why this file is an order of magnitude larger than any other
//! SDK's in this workspace, and it is one line of the pinned MinGW headers:**
//! `lib/libc/include/any-windows-any/winsock2.h:23` includes `<windows.h>`
//! unconditionally. On a Windows target curl's header closure therefore reaches
//! the whole Win32 SDK, and the names it contributes are the bulk of the list
//! below. No other SDK here has a header that opens a platform SDK the way
//! `curl.h:82` opens Winsock.
//!
//! ### Where that list came from, and what it is worth
//!
//! It is a deliberate over-approximation, assembled from two sources, and it is
//! worth being precise about both because a gap in it is not a cosmetic defect:
//! a name that reaches a translation and is missing from this list is routed,
//! and every load on that target then fails with `MissingSymbol`.
//!
//!   1. The pinned Zig distribution's own headers, read by walking the
//!     `#include` graph from the roots above through
//!     `lib/libc/include/generic-glibc`, `generic-musl`, `wasm-wasi-musl`,
//!     `any-windows-any`, `any-darwin-any`, `generic-freebsd`, `generic-netbsd`
//!     and `generic-openbsd` - the families the distribution holds for a target
//!     this package can be built for. Both sides of every `#if` were read, so a
//!     name a given target's configuration does not reach is in the list anyway
//!     and costs nothing, while a name it does reach is not missed.
//!   2. The `libc_noise` list `lua/runtime/graph.zig` carries, which was read
//!     out of real `zig translate-c` output rather than out of headers. Its
//!     roots - `<stdio.h>`, `<stdarg.h>`, `<stddef.h>`, `<limits.h>`,
//!     `<stdint.h>` - are a strict subset of curl's, so every name it holds is
//!     a name curl's translation can also carry. That is where the fortified
//!     `__*_chk` family and the `__isoc99_*`, `__isoc23_*`, `__mingw_*` and
//!     `_mingw_*` macro spellings come from: those are not declarations in any
//!     header, they are what a macro expansion turns into, and they are the part
//!     a header walk alone would miss.
//!
//! What this list is **not** is a transcript of a translation: no `zig
//! translate-c` run has happened here, so a name a specific target's
//! translation declares and that neither source above reaches would be missing.
//! Every other SDK in this workspace built its list by running the translation
//! over the whole published platform matrix and reading the externs out; curl's
//! is PENDING-VERIFICATION against that same treatment, and the driver's first
//! `zig build test` on a non-x86_64 target is what would confirm or refute it.
//! Names in the list that no translation declares are inert - `generate.zig`
//! consults it per declaration and a name it never meets simply never matches.
//!
//! ### The list is comma-separated, and every line ends with one
//!
//! `runtime/generate.zig` reads these lists through
//! `std.mem.splitScalar(u8, list, ',')` followed by a whitespace trim, and a
//! Zig multiline string literal concatenates its lines with nothing between
//! them. A line that does not end in a comma therefore glues its last name to
//! the first name of the next line into one token, and that token matches
//! neither declaration: both real names are lost and both get routed. The
//! commas are load-bearing, not decoration, and with a list this long a single
//! missing one is enough to break a load on one platform only.

const std = @import("std");

/// The prefix `runtime/sync.c` is compiled with.
///
/// `runtime/library.zig` declares the same prefix in the symbol names it binds:
/// `curl_rt_lock`, `curl_rt_unlock`, `curl_rt_wait`, `curl_rt_broadcast` and
/// `curl_rt_thread_self` come from `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=curl_rt` below, because
/// `runtime/sync.h:16-18` pastes the prefix into the symbol name rather than
/// using it as a string. The two must agree, and a build where they do not
/// fails to link, which is the whole error report: there is no runtime path
/// that could hide it, and no load that could fail more quietly.
///
/// The prefix is what keeps one SDK's mutex out of another SDK's address space.
/// `runtime/sync.c` defines its five functions weakly, so two translation units
/// that both compiled it share one mutex per prefix; two different prefixes
/// mean two different symbols and therefore two independent mutexes, which is
/// exactly the arrangement a process that loads two SDKs needs.
///
/// A build that needs a second, independent instance of this SDK in one binary
/// should change this value *and* the prefix in `runtime/library.zig`, and then
/// give the second instance its own copy of both files. Nothing else in the
/// package assumes one instance: the loader's table and its attempt record are
/// per configured module, and the C library behind it is opened with
/// `RTLD_LOCAL`, so a second instance's symbols cannot be found by the first
/// one's lookups.
const sync_prefix = "curl_rt";

